[Music]  a heart for his wife julia  the only goodbye alexayna valni could  manage before heading to prison  earlier he told the court the charges  against him were fabricated  a way of intimidating his supporters  all of these officers and this cage it's  not a show of strength  it shows their weakness they are weak  and they cannot jail thousands or even  millions of people  outside the cavalry arrived early  lines of riot police stood in front of  the court  not to keep navalny in but to keep his  supporters out  as the day progressed police detained  hundreds of navalny supporters  who'd come to show their solidarity  age was no barrier this woman stood her  ground in defiance  as night fell on moscow the protests  grew  but so did the presence of police in  riot gear  and the arrests  detention is a price these protesters  are willing to pay  to speak their mind  i don't want my children to grow up in  the same conditions i did  alex say is trying to do something at  least  navalny has just been wrongly convicted  the country will never be free if the  authorities and the courts behave like  this  it looks like they will soon start  beating us with their batons again  but we came out so that our children  won't be beaten with batons in the  future  soon enough the police proved him right  their batons directed indiscriminately  at anyone in the crowd  navalny supporters are taking risks in  the hope the next generation won't have  to  let's give more now we are joined by  andreas nick a member of the german  bundestag and the governing  conservatives he heads germany's  delegation  to the parliamentary assembly of the  council of europe welcome to the program  and thank you so much for joining us  the german government has condemned the  jail sentence for alexi navalny  is that enough  it is an important first step i think we  are very  concerned but also very impressed by the  courageous way that russian civil  society  protests against this development it  shows how much  pressure how much tension is there in  russia it is a domestic issue in the in  the first place  but it is an issue that has an important  impact on all international obligations  that russia has entered to whether it's  in the council of europe in the osce or  in other structures  and and that is why it is an important  element of international  obligations and and contractual  obligations and that's what we are  pursuing the verdict on which mr navalny  was  detained again was already  rendered obsolete by the european court  of human rights a couple of years ago  and that is something that the council  of europe in all its  institutional directions will have to  deal with going  in the next few weeks and it is uh  the obvious point of uh addressing this  because  russia is violating international  obligations that  the russian federation has entered okay  so  you say that the condemnations are an  important first step what is the second  step because we know that the eu for  example has already imposed targeted  sanctions because of  the poisoning of alexi navalny so what  should come  next and you know if the second step is  sanctions in your opinion can they  really make a difference  i think it is an important next step to  send a double message to russia both in  terms of the government  as in terms of the society that we are  strictly looking at the adherence to  constitutional obligations to  international obligations when it comes  to the freedom of speech to the  freedom of assembly to the rule of law  to  compliance with the judgments of the  european court of human rights  to which russia is a contractual party  but that at the same we are considering  all options  to increasing the pressure  and make clear also to the russian  leadership that there is  a price to be paid that there is a cost  to this behavior  and this is going to unfold over the  next couple of weeks should one of those  costs be  gas exports because earlier this week  france's european affairs minister  renewed pressure on germany to stop the  nordstrom 2 gas pipeline project  um it of course is scheduled to bring  russian gas to germany  um they're they're they're uh saying  that this should be a response the  cancelling of the project should be a  response to the navalny case  does germany need to seriously consider  doing more  in order to pull this project  i think we should narrow this discussion  to the completion  of the construction of nordstrom 2 i  think if we are considering the whole  set of options we should be talking  about asset freezes we should be talking  about travel bans  and we could have a very serious and  honest debate about  the energy relationships of everyone  with russia in this context  but i think for this debate to be honest  and meaningful i think everything has to  be on the table it is not about  bilateralizing this into a german  russian issue then we need to talk about  a 30 billion dollar of u.s oil  purchases of crude oil from russia we  need we would also need to talk about  gas export gas imports in central  eastern europe  i think to bilateralize it and to just  talk about one specific issue i think  is not uh meaningful it's just abusing  the nevada case for  certain political and commercial  interests there are some in your party  and i'd just like to bring in a quote  right now um from norbert rutkin for  example who's  who's as you know has been quite vocal  on this issue he's he said that  going ahead with the north stream two  project would and i'm quoting here  be the ultimate confirmation for  vladimir putin that he is pursuing  exactly the right policy  because the west is doing nothing at  least in the areas  that interest him i mean what message  does it send to putin  that if this project does indeed go  ahead that he can silence his critics  and  there will be no real consequences no  that's not what i said i think  i think there's always been a  significant scepticism towards this  project  in my own party the different views from  the economic and from the foreign policy  side  uh but given and i was never arguing  for this project at the outset but we  have to see where we are just briefly  before completion of a construction  for which all permits are in place this  would probably trigger billions of  dollars in  damage payments i'm not sure we should  be wanting that  to trigger but if we are if we are  talking about putting a freeze of  oil and gas purchases from russia  altogether in the west we will probably  have a serious conversation on that  but we should not uh allow that to be  just being bilateralized and i think if  you go back to  a very smart analysis that secretary of  state lincoln has  written more than 30 years ago  about a former dispute on  gas imports from siberia i think we  could have a  good basis with the new yes  administration to come to common ground  in addressing that but i think we should  not only talk about this commercial  aspect we should make absolutely clear  russia is not in compliance with its  obligations on the issue international  law under  its membership in the council of europe  and the osce and we need to see that we  exert maximum pressure and use all the  instruments available in these  institutions  to improve the situation of those in  russia mr navalny and the process has  detained  and those protesting in the streets we  thank you so much for joining us this  morning um  to to to share that view andreas nick  a member of the german bundestag and the  governing conservatives also head of  germany's delegation to the  parliamentary assembly of the council of  europe we appreciate it  thank you  you

